  "worked_solution": "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-3$, $x=-1,$ and $x=1.$\n\n- At $x=-3$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}f(x)=-2=f(-3).\\,\u2713\n$$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -2=f(-1).\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(1).\\,\u2713\n$$\n\nTherefore, from **left to right**, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at\n$x=-3$ and $x=1.$",
